TASKBAR CONTROL - QUICK GUIDE

Taskbar Control lets you choose which running apps have a taskbar button.
Hidden apps stay open. Their existing system tray icons remain available,
and supported apps remain accessible through Alt+Tab.

QUICK START
1. Open TaskbarControl.exe or its desktop/Start menu shortcut.
2. Choose Pick a window, then click the app you want to hide from the taskbar.
   Picking hides its taskbar button immediately. Press Escape to cancel.
3. Turn that app's Show switch ON whenever you want its button back.

ADDING APPS
Pick a window
  Select an open app without needing to find its executable. The selection
  click is intercepted so it does not press a button inside the selected app.

Add executable
  Browse for one or more .exe files. Added apps start with Show ON; turn it
  OFF to hide their taskbar buttons when they run. Adding an app does not
  launch it or close it.

Add running apps
  Add recognized open applications to the list. Existing choices are kept.
  Turn Show OFF for whichever apps you want hidden.

Add folder
  Scan a folder and its subfolders for .exe files. This is convenient for a
  collection of portable utilities. New entries start with Show ON. Large
  folders may also add installers, updaters, and other helper executables.

Saved folders
  View or remove folders you previously chose to scan. Removing a folder
  does not remove the app entries already discovered inside it.

Rescan folders
  Check saved folders for newly added executables. Existing show/hide
  choices are preserved. Use Cancel scan to stop an ongoing scan.

MANAGING THE APP LIST
Show switch
  ON: show the app's taskbar button. OFF: hide its taskbar button.
  This controls visibility; it does not stop the app or prevent it launching.

Search / Only hidden apps
  Search app names, executable paths, or app identities. Only hidden apps
  filters the list to entries whose Show switch is OFF.

Remove selected
  Remove selected rules from Taskbar Control. Their buttons are restored.
  The programs themselves are not uninstalled or deleted.

Show all apps
  Turn every Show switch ON. Entries remain in the list.

Pause hiding
  Temporarily restore taskbar buttons without forgetting which apps you hid.
  Turn Pause hiding OFF to resume applying those choices.

WINDOW, TRAY, AND STARTUP
Minimize / Close
  Both put Taskbar Control in the system tray and keep your rules active.
  Open the EXE or a shortcut again to bring back the existing window, or
  double-click its tray icon. It does not create another running copy.

Hide my taskbar icon
  Hide Taskbar Control's own taskbar button while keeping tray access.
  This separate option can also remove Taskbar Control itself from Alt+Tab.

Start with Windows
  Start quietly in the tray when you sign in. Setup lets you choose this
  before opening the app; the same toggle remains available in the app.

Exit completely
  Right-click the tray icon and choose Exit and restore taskbar buttons.

APPEARANCE
Use the theme menu in the top-right corner to select Dark theme or Light
theme. The choice is saved automatically, along with your app rules.

ERROR LOG AND DIAGNOSTICS
The error log below the controls shows recent problems in plain language,
with suggested next steps. Copy details copies the original error codes for
troubleshooting. Clear log clears the history; it does not change your rules.
The log is kept in memory and cleared when Taskbar Control exits.

Diagnostics provides a more detailed report and a Reconnect taskbar option.
Reports are generated locally. Review copied details before sharing them.
Some Windows taskbar versions or layouts are unsupported; the app leaves
affected buttons visible instead of using a method that removes Alt+Tab.

UNINSTALLING
Open the installation folder and double-click Uninstaller.exe or the
Uninstall Taskbar Control shortcut. You can also uninstall through Windows
Settings > Apps > Installed apps, or the Start menu uninstall shortcut if
you selected Start menu shortcuts during setup.

The uninstaller requests normal app exit and restores taskbar buttons. It
removes installed app files, its shortcuts, and its matching startup entry.
Saved rules and unrelated files are kept. If an older running version needs
to be closed manually, use Exit and restore taskbar buttons in its tray menu.

SAVED SETTINGS
Rules and preferences are stored in your Windows account at:
%LOCALAPPDATA%\TaskbarControl\settings.json
Updating or reinstalling the app preserves them. Download packages contain
no saved personal rules. See PRIVACY.md for privacy details and
Estimated System Resource Use.txt for a performance baseline.
